From 1bba69478cf69b4264d54bd893e9d25c76f0cc04 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 01:37:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] provenance: catch the silent trailer-block break instead of
 only reporting it

'provenance --strict' reported 12 commits as LOOSE-ONLY without saying what was wrong with
them, and the cause turned out to be a single undocumented git rule rather than carelessness:
git ends a trailer block at the first line that is not trailer-shaped, so a WRAPPED value whose
continuation starts in column 0 invalidates every trailer in the block - including the ones
above it. The message still reads correctly to a human, so nothing announces the failure. Six
of today's own commits tripped it.

Three changes, none of which touch the engine:

- provenance --strict now prints the actual cause and a wrong/right example instead of only
  naming the rule it applied.
- CONTRIBUTING/COMMIT-TRAILERS.md documents the three trailers, the wrapping rule, and why
  UNMEASURED is a legal value while a missing trailer is not.
- .githooks/commit-msg rejects a commit whose trailers are present but do not parse, by
  running git's own parser rather than a regex of our own. Opt in with
  'git config core.hooksPath .githooks'.

The hook was tested against all three classes it must separate, not just the failing one: an
unindented continuation (rejected), the indented form the fix produces (accepted), and a
message with no trailers at all (accepted, since presence is provenance --strict's job).

The 12 existing LOOSE-ONLY commits are deliberately NOT rewritten. Their provenance is present
and correct for a reader, and rewriting messages to satisfy a parser would invalidate the
series' verified byte-identical replay for no gain in the information itself. Recorded as a
known gap in the new document.

Origin: ArifiLabs lane-110D, public-release bar
Measured-effect: tooling and documentation only, no engine behaviour changed. Hook verified
  on three crafted messages covering reject/accept/no-op.
